    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-25821.s5">
        <fig id="article-25821.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Anatomy of Neurons. Panel A&#x000a0;shows 2&#x000a0;connected neurons. Each neuron has a soma containing a nucleus, an axon, and a dendritic tree. A single synapse (red circle) forms where an axon of one neuron (black) connects to the dendrite, soma, or axon of another neuron (blue). Panel B illustrates&#x000a0;a magnified view of a single synapse. Arrival of an action potential at the presynaptic terminal triggers calcium-mediated release of neurotransmitters from synaptic vesicles into the synaptic cleft. Neurotransmitters diffuse across the cleft and activate postsynaptic receptors. Contributed by K Aubrey, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Aubrey__Synapse__Figure__1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-25821.s6">
        <fig id="article-25821.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Action Potential Phases. This illustration depicts rapid depolarization, during which voltage-gated sodium channels open and the membrane potential rises; repolarization, marked by potassium efflux restoring negativity; and hyperpolarization, in which the membrane potential temporarily becomes more negative than the resting level. The resting membrane potential is &#x02212;70 mV, representing the steady-state baseline. The&#x000a0;threshold at &#x02212;55 mV (purple dashed line) indicates the membrane potential required to initiate an action potential. Used with permission from OpenStax under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Action_Potential" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-25821.s7">
        <fig id="article-25821.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ionic Channel Activity During Action Potentials. The table displays whether voltage-gated sodium and potassium channels are open, closed, or closing at specific physiological milestones. Key phases such as hyperpolarization and the absolute refractory period are linked to specific channel configurations. Contributed by Forshing Lui MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Neuron__Action__Potential" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
